.TH FALSE 1 2026-10-31 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
false \- do nothing, unsuccessfully
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B false
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B false
exits with status 1.
It does nothing else: it reads no input, writes no output, and ignores
its arguments.
It does not use the standard streams, so a closed one makes no
difference.
This
.B false
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Nothing on the command line is an option.
.BR \-\-help ,
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-
are ignored like any other argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B false
reads no environment variables.
The startup code shared by all applets reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
and may trim a trailing semicolon from
.BR PATH .
None of these changes what
.B false
does; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 1
Always.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the exit status of
.BR false :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ false; echo $?
1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a command only when the previous one fails:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ false || echo failed
failed
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike the GNU
.BR false ,
this one has no
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-\-version
and never prints anything.
.B busybox \-\-help false
prints the busybox version and
.B No help available
on standard output, and exits with status 0.
.PP
In the kit's shell,
.B false
is a builtin, so typing it does not start the applet.
Applets that run a command run this applet inside their own process, as
.B find \-exec
does, or start
.I busybox.exe
to run it, as
.B env
does.
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
can make them run a program from
.B PATH
instead; see
.BR busybox (1).
From
.BR cmd.exe ,
the launcher
.I false.exe
or
.B busybox false
runs it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR env (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR true (1)
